  <main>

    <div class="hero">
      <div class="tag">Migration Journal</div>
      <h1>Monolith →<br><span>Microservices</span></h1>
      <p>A journal of the full migration from a single <code>server.js</code> to a 7-container Docker architecture — what was built, what was decided, and what comes next.</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <div class="meta-item"><strong>September 2026</strong>Migration completed</div>
        <div class="meta-item"><strong>7 services</strong>All containerised</div>
        <div class="meta-item"><strong>Docker Hub</strong>Images ready to push</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── WHY WE MIGRATED ── -->
    <section class="section" id="context">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">1</div>
        <h2>Why We Migrated</h2>
      </div>

      <p>The original project was a single <code>server.js</code> at the root of the repo. It handled everything: Telegram authentication, group listing, content retrieval, and a stub for downloading. The Angular UI was under <code>modules/user-interface/</code> and was started alongside the backend with <code>concurrently</code> via <code>npm run dev</code>.</p>

      <p>This worked fine while the project was small. The migration was triggered when the scope grew to include <strong>real file downloading</strong> (saving Telegram media to the local filesystem) and <strong>Google Drive integration</strong> (upload / download). These two concerns are genuinely independent of Telegram auth and reading — no code needs to be shared between them, they have different dependencies, and they could reasonably need to scale differently in the future.</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title" style="color:var(--accent5);">Before — Monolith</div>
          <p>One <code>server.js</code>. One crash takes down everything — auth, reading, downloading all at once. All future services would accumulate in the same file. No way to develop or deploy one feature independently.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title" style="color:var(--accent3);">After — Microservices</div>
          <p>7 containers, each doing one thing. Auth can be restarted without disrupting the read service. New services can be added without touching existing ones. Each has its own <code>Dockerfile</code>, <code>package.json</code>, and deploy lifecycle.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── BEFORE & AFTER ── -->
    <section class="section" id="before-after">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">2</div>
        <h2>Before & After</h2>
      </div>

      <table class="compare">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Before (Monolith)</th><th>After (Microservices)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Entry point</td>
            <td>Root <code>server.js</code></td>
            <td><code>services/*/server.js</code> — 7 files</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Starting the app</td>
            <td><code>npm run dev</code> (concurrently)</td>
            <td><code>npm run dev</code> → <code>docker compose up</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>UI serving</td>
            <td><code>ng serve</code> dev server</td>
            <td>nginx serving compiled static files</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Auth code</td>
            <td><code>modules/user-authorization/auth.service.js</code></td>
            <td><code>services/auth-service/server.js</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Download support</td>
            <td>TODO stub</td>
            <td><code>services/telegram-download-service</code> — implemented</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google integration</td>
            <td>Not started</td>
            <td><code>services/google-upload-service</code> + <code>google-download-service</code> — scaffolded</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Fault isolation</td>
            <td>None — one process</td>
            <td>Each service is isolated; one crash doesn't cascade</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Image distribution</td>
            <td>N/A — run from source</td>
            <td>7 Docker Hub images, buildable with <code>make build</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <!-- ── TIMELINE ── -->
    <section class="section" id="timeline">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">3</div>
        <h2>Migration Timeline</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="timeline">

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Start</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Decided to split into microservices</strong> — scope expanded to include downloading and Google Drive. Clear boundaries existed between auth, reading, downloading, and Google — each should be its own service.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Created <code>services/</code> directory at root</strong> — 7 sub-folders: <code>auth-service</code>, <code>telegram-read-service</code>, <code>telegram-download-service</code>, <code>google-upload-service</code>, <code>google-download-service</code>, <code>api-gateway</code>, <code>ui-service</code>. Each got its own <code>server.js</code>, <code>package.json</code>, <code>Dockerfile</code>, and <code>.dockerignore</code>.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Migrated auth + read logic</strong> from the old monolith into <code>auth-service</code> and <code>telegram-read-service</code>. Ported Telegram GramJS client, session handling, group listing, and content retrieval.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Built <code>telegram-download-service</code></strong> — implemented the download endpoint that was previously a TODO stub. Downloads Telegram media to configurable local paths (Desktop, Downloads, or Custom) via Docker volume mounts.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Scaffolded <code>google-upload-service</code> and <code>google-download-service</code></strong> — basic Express servers with health endpoints. Google API integration is in progress.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Built the API Gateway</strong> — single entry point using <code>http-proxy-middleware</code>. Routes <code>/auth/*</code>, <code>/groups/*</code>, <code>/download</code>, <code>/google/upload</code>, <code>/google/download</code> to the correct service. Strips path prefixes via <code>pathRewrite</code>.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Moved Angular UI into <code>ui-service</code></strong> — Angular app is compiled to static files (<code>dist/</code>) and served by nginx. Nginx also proxies <code>/api/*</code> calls to the API gateway, so the browser never needs to know the gateway address.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Wrote <code>docker-compose.yml</code></strong> — defines all 7 services, their ports, volumes, environment variables, networks, and restart policies. All services join a shared <code>app-network</code> bridge network.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Wrote <code>Makefile</code></strong> — <code>make build</code>, <code>make push</code>, <code>make up</code>, <code>make down</code>, <code>make save</code>, <code>make load</code>, <code>make clean</code>. Tagged each image with both <code>:latest</code> and the git short SHA for traceability.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot info"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label info">Step</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Deleted old <code>modules/</code> folder</strong> — no longer needed. All code lives in <code>services/</code>. Removed the old root <code>server.js</code> too.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="timeline-item">
          <div class="timeline-dot fix"></div>
          <div class="timeline-label fix">Done</div>
          <div class="timeline-text"><strong>Full stack builds and runs</strong> — <code>npm run dev</code> (or <code>make up</code>) starts all 7 containers. App is accessible at <code>localhost:4200</code>. Full error journal documented separately in <a href="Build-Journal.html" style="color:var(--accent)">Build-Journal.html</a>.</div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 7 SERVICES ── -->
    <section class="section" id="services">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">4</div>
        <h2>The 7 Services</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Each service is self-contained — its own <code>package.json</code>, <code>Dockerfile</code>, and <code>server.js</code>. Services communicate with each other only via HTTP over the Docker internal network. No shared code, no shared database.</p>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">auth-service — :3001</div>
        <p>Owns the Telegram session. Handles phone login, OTP, 2FA, and session persistence. The session string is stored as an environment variable and loaded on startup. All other services that need an authenticated Telegram client must go through auth first — but in practice only auth and telegram-read share the session.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">telegram-read-service — :3002</div>
        <p>Reads from Telegram. Returns groups/channels, profile photos, and paginated message/file content. Also creates its own Telegram client using the same session string from the environment — it doesn't call auth-service at runtime.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">telegram-download-service — :3003</div>
        <p>Downloads Telegram media files to the local filesystem. Accepts a message reference (chat ID + message ID) and writes the file to a configurable destination. Three destinations are available: Desktop, Downloads, or a repo-local Custom folder — each mounted as a Docker volume so files land on the host machine, not inside the container.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">google-upload-service — :3004</div>
        <p>Scaffolded. Will receive a file path and upload it to Google Drive using the Google API. OAuth credential handling is the main work remaining.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">google-download-service — :3005</div>
        <p>Scaffolded. Will receive a Google Drive file ID and download it to the local filesystem. Depends on the same OAuth setup as the upload service.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">api-gateway — :3000</div>
        <p>The single entry point. Routes by path prefix to the correct service using <code>http-proxy-middleware</code>. Strips path prefixes via <code>pathRewrite</code> so downstream services don't know they're behind a gateway. Has no business logic of its own.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-title">ui-service — :4200 (→ :80)</div>
        <p>Angular app compiled to static files and served by nginx. The nginx config handles Angular's client-side routing (via <code>try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html</code>) and proxies <code>/api/*</code> calls to the API gateway. The compiled output is ~2MB; the final Docker image is ~25MB using nginx:alpine.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── API GATEWAY ── -->
    <section class="section" id="gateway">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">5</div>
        <h2>API Gateway Design</h2>
      </div>

      <p>The gateway's routing table maps URL prefixes to services. Inside Docker, service names resolve as DNS hostnames on the <code>app-network</code>.</p>

      <pre><code><span class="c">// Routing table</span>
<span class="c">// Outside Docker → gateway reads from env vars</span>
<span class="c">// Inside Docker  → service names resolve via Docker DNS</span>

<span class="n">app.use</span>(<span class="s">'/auth'</span>,            proxy({ target: AUTH_SERVICE_URL,    pathRewrite: { <span class="s">'^/auth'</span>: <span class="s">''</span> } }));
<span class="n">app.use</span>(<span class="s">'/groups'</span>,          proxy({ target: READ_SERVICE_URL,    pathRewrite: { <span class="s">'^/groups'</span>: <span class="s">'/groups'</span> } }));
<span class="n">app.use</span>(<span class="s">'/download'</span>,        proxy({ target: DOWNLOAD_SERVICE_URL, pathRewrite: { <span class="s">'^/download'</span>: <span class="s">'/download'</span> } }));
<span class="n">app.use</span>(<span class="s">'/google/upload'</span>,   proxy({ target: GUPLOAD_SERVICE_URL, pathRewrite: { <span class="s">'^/google/upload'</span>: <span class="s">'/upload'</span> } }));
<span class="n">app.use</span>(<span class="s">'/google/download'</span>, proxy({ target: GDOWNLOAD_URL,      pathRewrite: { <span class="s">'^/google/download'</span>: <span class="s">'/download'</span> } }));</code></pre>

      <div class="callout info">
        <div class="callout-icon">💡</div>
        <div class="callout-body">
          <strong>pathRewrite for /auth is different</strong>
          The auth service was originally written without any <code>/auth</code> prefix on its routes — it just has <code>/send-code</code>, <code>/sign-in</code>, etc. The gateway strips <code>/auth</code> before forwarding so the auth service doesn't need to be aware it's behind a gateway. The other services keep their prefixes.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── DOWNLOAD SERVICE ── -->
    <section class="section" id="download">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">6</div>
        <h2>Telegram Download Service</h2>
      </div>

      <p>The download service was the primary motivation for the microservices split. It's where the project moved from read-only to actually useful.</p>

      <h3>How it works</h3>
      <p>The Angular UI sends a <code>POST /download</code> request with a message reference and a destination. The service:</p>
      <ol style="padding-left:20px;color:var(--muted);font-size:14px;margin-bottom:16px;">
        <li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Connects to Telegram with the session string from env</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Fetches the specified message</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Streams the media file using GramJS <code>downloadMedia</code></li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Writes the file to the requested destination path</li>
        <li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Returns the saved file path in the response</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Download destination strategy</h3>
      <p>Files need to land on the <em>host machine</em> (your Mac), not inside the container (which is ephemeral). Docker volume mounts solve this: the host directories are mounted into the container at known paths.</p>

      <pre><code><span class="c"># docker-compose.yml — telegram-download-service volumes</span>
volumes:
  - ~/Desktop:/host/Desktop          <span class="c"># your Mac Desktop</span>
  - ~/Downloads:/host/Downloads      <span class="c"># your Mac Downloads folder</span>
  - ./downloads/Custom:/host/Custom  <span class="c"># repo-local custom folder</span>

<span class="c"># Environment variables tell the service where each mount lives</span>
environment:
  DOWNLOADS_DESKTOP:   /host/Desktop
  DOWNLOADS_DOWNLOADS: /host/Downloads
  DOWNLOADS_CUSTOM:    /host/Custom</code></pre>

      <p>The service maps the requested destination name (<code>"Desktop"</code>, <code>"Downloads"</code>, <code>"Custom"</code>) to the corresponding container path. Files written there appear immediately on the host machine.</p>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <div class="callout-icon">🔍</div>
        <div class="callout-body">
          <strong>Why not write directly to the container filesystem?</strong>
          Containers are ephemeral — if the container restarts or is removed, everything inside it is gone. Volume mounts persist data on the host regardless of what happens to the container.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── UI SERVICE ── -->
    <section class="section" id="ui">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">7</div>
        <h2>UI Service</h2>
      </div>

      <p>In development the Angular UI was served with <code>ng serve</code> — a dev server with hot reload. In the Docker setup, that's replaced with a 3-stage build:</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Stage 1 — Install deps</div>
          <p><code>npm ci</code> including devDependencies (Angular CLI, TypeScript) needed to compile. Not included in the final image.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Stage 2 — Compile</div>
          <p><code>ng build</code> compiles TypeScript + templates → HTML/CSS/JS bundles in <code>dist/</code>. Node.js not included in final image.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Stage 3 — Serve</div>
          <p>nginx:alpine serves the static files from <code>dist/</code>. Also proxies <code>/api/*</code> to the gateway. Final image ~25MB.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>nginx config highlights</h3>
      <pre><code><span class="c"># Angular client-side routing — refresh /group/123 works</span>
location / {
    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
}

<span class="c"># API proxy — browser calls /api/groups, nginx forwards to gateway</span>
<span class="c"># The browser never hardcodes localhost:3000</span>
location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://api-gateway:3000/;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- ── DECISIONS ── -->
    <section class="section" id="decision-delete-modules">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">8</div>
        <h2>Decision — Delete <code>modules/</code> Entirely</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="decision-block">
        <div class="decision-header">
          <span class="decision-badge">Decision</span>
          <span class="decision-title">Removed modules/ folder and root server.js; all code moves to services/</span>
        </div>
        <div class="decision-body">
          <p><strong>What was there:</strong> <code>modules/</code> contained <code>user-authorization</code>, <code>user-interface</code>, <code>telegram-read</code>, <code>telegram-download</code>, <code>google-upload</code>, <code>google-download</code> — old monolith structure.</p>
          <p><strong>Why delete it:</strong> Keeping both <code>modules/</code> and <code>services/</code> in the repo would be confusing — two parallel structures, different stages of the same feature. Since the new architecture is the target state, the old structure was deleted to prevent any ambiguity about which code is authoritative.</p>
          <p><strong>Risk:</strong> Low. The new services had been fully written and tested before the deletion. The old modules were referenced only by the old root <code>server.js</code>, which was also being replaced.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="decision-download-paths">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">9</div>
        <h2>Decision — Three Download Destinations</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="decision-block">
        <div class="decision-header">
          <span class="decision-badge">Decision</span>
          <span class="decision-title">Desktop / Downloads / Custom — configurable via env vars, mounted as volumes</span>
        </div>
        <div class="decision-body">
          <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Where should downloaded files go? The container's filesystem is ephemeral. Users want files on their actual machine.</p>
          <p><strong>Approach chosen:</strong> Three Docker volume mounts give the user three practical choices:</p>
          <ul class="bullet">
            <li><strong>Desktop</strong> — easiest to find, good for quick testing</li>
            <li><strong>Downloads</strong> — familiar system folder, good default</li>
            <li><strong>Custom</strong> — a folder inside the repo (<code>./downloads/Custom</code>) for keeping project downloads together</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The destination is passed in the POST request body by the Angular UI. The service maps the destination name to the corresponding container path via env vars, so paths can be overridden without code changes.</p>
          <p><strong>Future:</strong> A free-text custom path input could be added to the UI so users can specify any local directory — they'd just need to add the corresponding volume mount to <code>docker-compose.yml</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="decision-docker-debug">
      <div class="section-header">
        <div class="section-number">10</div>
        <h2>Decision — Debug Ports in docker-compose.yml</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="decision-block">
        <div class="decision-header">
          <span class="decision-badge">Decision</span>
          <span class="decision-title">Each service exposes a secondary debug port (9228–9233)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="decision-body">
          <p>Each service exposes a debug port alongside its main port (e.g. auth-service: <code>3001</code> + <code>9230</code>). This allows Node.js's built-in inspector to be attached from VS Code's "Attach to Node" launch config without restarting the container.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="c"># In docker-compose.yml</span>
ports:
  - "3001:3001"   <span class="c"># HTTP</span>
  - "9230:9230"   <span class="c"># Node.js debugger (inspector)</span>

<span class="c"># In the service's Dockerfile or start command:</span>
CMD ["node", "--inspect=0.0.0.0:9230", "server.js"]</code></pre>
          <p>The <code>0.0.0.0</code> binding is necessary — the default <code>127.0.0.1</code> only listens inside the container and is unreachable from the host.</p>
          <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:16px;">
            <div class="callout-icon">⚠️</div>
            <div class="callout-body">
              <strong>Remove debug ports before production</strong>
              Exposing the Node.js inspector means anyone who can reach the port can execute arbitrary code inside the container. Debug ports should only be mapped in local development compose files, not in production deployments.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

